---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import CtaBand from '../../components/CtaBand.astro';
import { SITE } from '../../lib/site';
---
<Base title="Admission requirements & process" description="How international students apply to medical universities in Ukraine: the four application stages, documents usually requested, and what we do and do not do.">
  <div class="page-head"><div class="wrap"><span class="eyebrow">Admissions guide</span><h1>Admission requirements and application process</h1><p>What the process involves, which documents universities typically ask for, and exactly what we do and do not do.</p></div></div>
  <Breadcrumbs items={[{ label: 'Admissions' }]} />
  <section style="padding-top:1rem"><div class="wrap prose">
    <h2>Who sets the requirements</h2>
    <p>Entry requirements, document lists and deadlines are set by each university and by Ukrainian regulations for international students. They change from year to year. On every university profile we show the published requirement with its source and the date we last checked it; where we have not yet confirmed a requirement it is marked “to be confirmed”.</p>

    <h2 id="stages">The four stages of an application</h2>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Enquiry.</strong> You tell us your course, preferred universities and background. Nothing is sent to any university.</li>
      <li><strong>Draft application.</strong> You complete our online application and upload documents at your own pace. Still nothing is sent to a university.</li>
      <li><strong>Application submitted to Ukraine Medical Admissions.</strong> You confirm your application to us. We review it, request anything missing and advise on eligibility.</li>
      <li><strong>Official submission to the university.</strong> Only once you have confirmed, and only through the university's authorised procedure. We will never tell you an application has reached a university unless that step has actually been completed.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2 id="readiness">Are you eligible? How the readiness check works</h2>
    <p>Entry requirements are specific to a university, a programme, your own school-leaving qualification and the intake year. There is no universal list of subjects or grades for “medicine in Ukraine”. We record each requirement exactly as the university publishes it — subject, minimum grade and grading scale, entrance examination and its format, language requirement — with the source and the date read. Only verified requirements are used in a readiness check.</p>
    <p>When you start a candidate profile you tell us your qualification, the subjects you studied with their grades, and any results still outstanding. For each verified requirement of the programme you choose, the check shows one of four results: <strong>met</strong>, <strong>not met</strong>, <strong>unknown</strong> (the requirement is not yet verified, or your record lacks the detail) or <strong>pending evidence</strong> (your result is not out yet). It is a comparison with published requirements, not an admission decision, and it never says you are “eligible” or “ineligible”.</p>
    <p>If a requirement is not met, the profile explains the gap and, where a university publishes a preparatory route, what that route is — see below. You can start a profile with missing documents or incomplete results; nothing about it is final until you choose to submit.</p>

    <h2 id="preparatory">Preparatory and foundation routes</h2>
    <p>Many Ukrainian universities run a preparatory department for foreign applicants: usually a year of Ukrainian (sometimes English) language and science subjects, ending in a certificate. Each university publishes its own duration, subjects, language of instruction, fee and — most importantly — its own wording on what completing the course gives. We quote that wording verbatim on the profile once it has been verified, and never generalise it.</p>
    <p>A preparatory course improves academic preparation. It does not guarantee admission, progression or a place on any degree programme; the degree's own entry requirements still apply. No route is listed on this site until its official page has been read.</p>

    <h2 id="documents">Documents universities commonly request</h2>
    <p>The exact list is confirmed per university and intake. Typical items include:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Passport (valid for the period of study).</li>
      <li>School-leaving certificate and transcript, with subject grades (often biology and chemistry are required for medicine).</li>
      <li>For transfer or postgraduate applicants: prior academic transcripts.</li>
      <li>Medical certificate and, where required, specific test results.</li>
      <li>Passport-style photographs.</li>
      <li>Certified translations into Ukrainian where the university requires them.</li>
      <li>Legalisation or apostille of documents where required for your country.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Do not send original documents to anyone until the university's procedure asks for them.</p>
    <h3 id="checklist">How your document checklist works</h3>
    <p>Your profile keeps a checklist generated from the verified document requirements of the programme you choose. Each item moves through clear states: <strong>required</strong> → <strong>outstanding</strong> → <strong>uploaded</strong> (when secure uploads are available) → <strong>under review</strong> → <strong>clarification requested</strong> or <strong>accepted for processing</strong>. “Accepted for processing” means our reviewer found the item complete and legible for our own review; it never asserts that a document is authentic or that a university has accepted it.</p>
    <p class="note">Uploads are not yet available. At this stage we only ask which documents you already have and which you still need; do not e-mail scans of your passport or certificates.</p>

    <h2>Language of instruction</h2>
    <p>Some programmes are taught in English and some in Ukrainian; availability for international applicants varies by programme and year. We record the language only from the university's official programme page.</p>

    <h2>Timing</h2>
    <p>Intake dates and deadlines are published by each university. Because they vary, we show them on the university profile rather than here, each with a check date.</p>

    <h2 id="official-sites">Apply through official channels only</h2>
    <p>Some websites use a university's name in their domain without being the university. Every profile on this site links the university's official domain and, where we have found a look-alike site in search results, says so. Admission documents and payments go only through the university's own published procedure.</p>

    <h2>What we do not do</h2>
    <p>We do not guarantee admission, visas, scholarships, recognition of a qualification in any country, or employment. {SITE.disclaimer}</p>
    <p>Next: read the <a href="/tuition-fees/">tuition fees guide</a>, browse <a href="/universities/">universities</a>, or <a href="/apply/">start your application</a>.</p>
  </div></section>
  <CtaBand />
</Base>
